"""serverjack: a phone-friendly web front door to your home server.

    https://<host>/        -> this app (default 127.0.0.1:7680)
    https://<host>/term/   -> ttyd      (default 127.0.0.1:7681)

Everything it does ends in the same place: a command running inside a tmux
session, with a terminal on your screen. The landing page has three panels,
in the order you actually need them from a phone:

  1. Run a command  -- an agent told you to run `sudo apt install ffmpeg` and
     it can't do that itself. Paste it, tap Run: it starts in a new session in
     front of a login shell, so a sudo password prompt works and the output
     stays on screen afterwards.
  2. Sessions       -- list, open, kill; plus a plain new shell.
  3. Agents         -- an accordion with one collapsed row per coding CLI
     (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Copilot, Gemini). The row shows the tool
     and its state: not installed / installed but not logged in / ready, plus
     a pill for any server or daemon. Open one (they are exclusive, native
     <details name=...>, no JS) and the body offers one option row per way of
     starting it -- here in this terminal, with remote control, as a server,
     as a daemon -- each with a one-line note on the difference. Every button
     just launches a command in a session and shows you the terminal --
     install scripts print progress, login flows print a URL or a device code,
     remote-control servers print a QR code. serverjack never parses what a
     tool prints. The one exception is the Codex daemon, whose start/stop are
     quick non-interactive commands.

Opening a session goes to /s/<name>: a slim menu bar (session tabs, + new,
x close, pop-out, soft keys for phones) over ttyd in a frame at
/term/?arg=<name>; ttyd runs bin/tmux-attach.sh with that argument, which
attaches. The terminal only ever appears once you are inside a session.

Stdlib only, no dependencies. Runs as your user and talks to your normal tmux
server, so it sees the same sessions as any other login.

Configuration is by environment variables (see install.sh / README):
    SERVERJACK_LISTEN "tcp" (default) or "unix". tcp binds
                      127.0.0.1:$SERVERJACK_PORT and refuses any connection
                      whose other end is not owned by root (tailscaled) or by
                      us -- see peer_uid() below. unix binds
                      $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/serverjack/web.sock (fallback base
                      /tmp/serverjack-$UID), mode 0600, which the kernel
                      enforces instead; that mode needs `tailscale serve` to be
                      run as root once, because tailscale will not proxy to a
                      Unix socket for an unprivileged caller.
    SERVERJACK_PORT   listen port (127.0.0.1, tcp mode only) default 7680
    SERVERJACK_TRUST_UIDS
                      extra uids allowed to connect in tcp mode, comma-
                      separated -- a reverse proxy running as its own user
                      (nginx is often uid 101 or 33).
    SERVERJACK_TRUST_LOCAL
                      set to 1 to turn the peer-uid check off entirely. Only
                      if you know why: it hands every local account a shell.
    SERVERJACK_ALLOW  comma-separated tailnet logins (alice@github, ...).
                      When set, every request must carry a Tailscale-User-Login
                      header naming one of them or it gets a 403 page. The
                      header is `tailscale serve`'s, and it is trustworthy in
                      both modes because nothing but tailscaled and this
                      account can open a connection in the first place. Unset =
                      no check: every tailnet device that reaches the port gets
                      a shell.
    SERVERJACK_TITLE  page title                             default hostname
    SERVERJACK_DIRS   colon-separated dirs offered for new sessions
    SERVERJACK_TOOLS  comma-separated agent ids to show, in that order
                      (default: every tool in the registry below)
    SERVERJACK_TERM   URL path where ttyd is mounted         default /term/
    SERVERJACK_SSH    user@host for the "open in an SSH app" menu item,
                      or "off" to hide it   (default: the tailnet name)
    SERVERJACK_CONFIG config dir  (default ~/.config/serverjack)

Runtime dir: $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/serverjack (fallback /tmp/serverjack-$UID/
serverjack), created 0700 by whichever of serverjack / serverjack-ttyd starts
first. It holds "secret" (0600) -- 32 random bytes minted on first start -- and,
in unix mode, web.sock and ttyd.sock. Every session name gets a token,
HMAC-SHA256(secret, name)[:32], which the page puts in the terminal URL
(/term/?arg=<name>&arg=<token>). bin/tmux-attach.sh recomputes it and ALWAYS
requires it: ttyd has no idea who is on the other end, so without the token a
device turned away by SERVERJACK_ALLOW -- or, in tcp mode, anything that got to
ttyd's port -- could get a shell by loading the ttyd URL directly.

Agents come from BUILTIN_TOOLS below. To change one, or add your own, drop a
JSON *list* in $SERVERJACK_CONFIG/tools.json; entries are merged over the
built-in tool with the same id (field by field, except "server"/"daemon",
which replace wholesale), "hidden": true drops one, and unknown ids are
appended. Fields: id, label, bin, paths, docs, install, install_note, needs ("node"),
login, login_note, login_check, run, run_note, server{label,cmd,session,note,per_dir},
daemon{label,start,stop,pidfile,note}, actions[{label,cmd,note,dir}], hidden.
server.per_dir: true means one server per project directory (Claude's model):
each Start makes another tmux session named <session>-<dirname> and the card
lists every instance with its directory. Otherwise there is one session.
run_note, actions[].note, server.note and daemon.note are the one-line
explanations shown beside those options; keep them to a single sentence.
"paths" lists extra directories to look for "bin" in -- a coding CLI often
installs into a private bin dir that only your .bashrc adds to PATH; the ones
that exist are appended to the PATH used for the installed/login checks, the
daemon commands and every session serverjack starts.
Only id, label and bin are required. Example:

    [
      {"id": "opencode", "server": {"label": "Server for the mobile app",
        "cmd": "opencode serve --hostname 0.0.0.0", "session": "opencode-serve"}},
      {"id": "gemini", "hidden": true},
      {"id": "aider", "label": "Aider", "bin": "aider", "run": "aider",
       "install": "pipx install aider-chat"}
    ]

Every agent card submits one form to POST /tools, and the button that was
pressed carries the route in its value ("/tools/open", "/tools/action:0"...),
so a card can share one directory picker between several buttons. Those same
routes also work as plain endpoints of their own.

Security: no password of its own. Every button on this page runs a command as
the user serverjack runs as -- it is SSH with a nicer font. Only expose it
through something that authenticates (tailscale serve, an SSO reverse proxy, a
VPN); never `tailscale funnel` it. Other accounts on the same machine are kept
out two ways: in tcp mode every connection's owner is looked up in
/proc/net/tcp and refused unless it is root, us, or a uid in
SERVERJACK_TRUST_UIDS; in unix mode the socket is 0600 inside a 0700 directory
and the kernel does it. Identity, when you want it, comes from Tailscale:
SERVERJACK_ALLOW names the tailnet logins allowed in, and the terminal is
token-gated so it cannot be reached around the page. State-changing POSTs are
refused when the browser says they came from another site (Sec-Fetch-Site /
Origin), so a random web page can't drive it from a logged-in browser.
"""

import concurrent.futures
import hashlib
import hmac
import html
import json
import os
import re
import getpass
import shlex
import shutil
import socket
import socketserver
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer
from urllib.parse import parse_qs, quote, unquote, urlparse

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
LISTEN = (os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_LISTEN") or "tcp").strip().lower()
BIND = ("127.0.0.1", int(os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_PORT", "7680")))


def runtime_dir():
    """Where the sockets and the token secret live. Same expression as
    bin/serverjack-ttyd and bin/tmux-attach.sh use in shell:
        ${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/tmp/serverjack-$(id -u)}/serverjack
    Not a systemd RuntimeDirectory=: that would delete the directory (and the
    other unit's socket) whenever one of the two units stops."""
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR") or f"/tmp/serverjack-{os.getuid()}"
    d = os.path.join(base, "serverjack")
    os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
    os.chmod(d, 0o700)                 # private to this account, whoever made it
    return d


RUNTIME_DIR = runtime_dir()
WEB_SOCK = os.path.join(RUNTIME_DIR, "web.sock")


def _secret():
    """32 random bytes (hex) in <runtime dir>/secret, mode 0600, minted once."""
    p = os.path.join(RUNTIME_DIR, "secret")
    for _ in range(2):
        try:
            fd = os.open(p, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o600)
            with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as f:
                f.write(os.urandom(32).hex().encode())
        except FileExistsError:
            pass
        try:
            with open(p, "rb") as f:
                data = f.read().strip()
            if data:
                return data
        except OSError:
            pass
        try:
            os.unlink(p)               # empty/unreadable: start over once
        except OSError:
            pass
    raise SystemExit(f"cannot create {p}")


SECRET = _secret()


def session_token(name):
    """Unguessable per-session token. tmux-attach.sh recomputes it from the
    same secret, so only a URL this page handed out attaches a terminal."""
    return hmac.new(SECRET, name.encode(), hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()[:32]


ALLOW = [x.strip().lower() for x in (os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_ALLOW") or "").split(",") if x.strip()]

# --- who else on this machine may connect (tcp mode only) -------------------
#
# A localhost TCP port has no owner: any Linux account on the box can open
# 127.0.0.1:7680 and press the buttons, which is a shell as us. The kernel does
# record who owns each socket, though, so before routing anything we look the
# peer up in /proc/net/tcp and refuse a stranger. Allowed by default: root
# (that is tailscaled proxying for `tailscale serve`) and our own uid.
# SERVERJACK_TRUST_UIDS adds more -- a reverse proxy running as its own user.
# SERVERJACK_TRUST_LOCAL=1 turns the whole thing off; see the README.
# In unix mode none of this runs: the 0700 directory already says it.

TRUST_LOCAL = (os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_TRUST_LOCAL") or "").strip() not in ("", "0", "no", "false")
TRUST_UIDS = {int(x) for x in re.findall(r"\d+", os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_TRUST_UIDS") or "")}
ALLOWED_UIDS = {0, os.getuid()} | TRUST_UIDS
_warned_no_row = False


def _proc_addr(field):
    """"0100007F:1E08" -> ("127.0.0.1", 7680). Each 4-byte word of the address
    is little-endian; tcp6 rows are four such words, and an IPv4-mapped peer
    (::ffff:127.0.0.1) is normalised back to its IPv4 form so both files
    compare against the same thing."""
    hexip, _, hexport = field.partition(":")
    raw = bytes.fromhex(hexip)
    raw = b"".join(raw[i:i + 4][::-1] for i in range(0, len(raw), 4))
    fam = socket.AF_INET if len(raw) == 4 else socket.AF_INET6
    ip = socket.inet_ntop(fam, raw)
    if ip.startswith("::ffff:"):
        ip = ip[7:]
    return ip, int(hexport, 16)


def _norm(ip):
    return ip[7:] if ip.startswith("::ffff:") else ip


def peer_uid(conn):
    """uid owning the other end of this connection, or None if we can't tell.
    The peer's own row is the one whose local address is our remote address and
    whose remote address is our local one."""
    try:
        peer = conn.getpeername()
        ours = conn.getsockname()
    except OSError:
        return None
    want_local = (_norm(peer[0]), peer[1])
    want_remote = (_norm(ours[0]), ours[1])
    for path in ("/proc/net/tcp", "/proc/net/tcp6"):
        try:
            with open(path) as f:
                next(f, None)                      # header
                for line in f:
                    col = line.split()
                    if len(col) < 8:
                        continue
                    try:
                        if _proc_addr(col[1]) == want_local and _proc_addr(col[2]) == want_remote:
                            return int(col[7])
                    except (ValueError, OSError):
                        continue
        except OSError:
            continue
    return None


def peer_allowed(conn):
    """Fails CLOSED: a peer we cannot identify is a peer we don't serve."""
    global _warned_no_row
    if LISTEN != "tcp" or TRUST_LOCAL:
        return True
    uid = peer_uid(conn)
    if uid is None:
        if not _warned_no_row:
            _warned_no_row = True
            sys.stderr.write(
                "serverjack: cannot find the peer's socket in /proc/net/tcp -- refusing "
                "connections it cannot identify. Set SERVERJACK_TRUST_LOCAL=1 to disable "
                "the check, or SERVERJACK_LISTEN=unix to not need it.\n")
        return False
    return uid in ALLOWED_UIDS
TITLE = os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_TITLE") or socket.gethostname().split(".")[0]
TERM_PATH = os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_TERM", "/term/")
if not TERM_PATH.endswith("/"):
    TERM_PATH += "/"          # ttyd builds its ws URL relative to the page path


def _dirs():
    raw = os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_DIRS") or "~/projects:~/src:~/workspace:~"
    out = []
    for d in raw.split(":"):
        d = os.path.expanduser(d.strip())
        if d and os.path.isdir(d) and d not in out:
            out.append(d)
    if HOME not in out:
        out.append(HOME)      # last, so the picker defaults to a real project dir
    return out


DIR_ROOTS = _dirs()
CONFIG_DIR = os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_CONFIG") or os.path.join(
    os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME") or f"{HOME}/.config", "serverjack")
SHORTCUTS_FILE = os.path.join(CONFIG_DIR, "shortcuts.json")
TOOLS_FILE = os.path.join(CONFIG_DIR, "tools.json")


def _ssh_target():
    """user@host for 'connect over SSH instead' links. SERVERJACK_SSH overrides;
    otherwise the tailnet DNS name if tailscale is up, else the hostname."""
    t = os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_SSH", "").strip()
    if t == "off":
        return ""
    if t:
        return t
    host = ""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["tailscale", "status", "--json"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=5)
        if r.returncode == 0:
            host = (json.loads(r.stdout).get("Self") or {}).get("DNSName", "").rstrip(".")
    except (OSError, ValueError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass
    host = host or socket.getfqdn() or socket.gethostname()
    return f"{getpass.getuser()}@{host}"


SSH_TARGET = _ssh_target()


def ssh_cmd(name):
    return f"ssh -t {SSH_TARGET} tmux attach -t {shlex.quote(name)}" if SSH_TARGET else ""


PATH_ENV = os.environ.get("PATH") or f"{HOME}/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin"


